.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the native Substrate linker implementation for ELF inputs.
It links
.Dv ET_REL
objects and archives into relocatable, executable, and shared outputs.
It does not forward to external linker backends.
.Pp
When no target mode is forced,
.Nm
auto-selects i386 or x86-64 mode from the first compatible input.
.Pp
When installed through the host toolchain workflow described in
.Xr host_toolchain 7 ,
the installed
.Xr cc 1
driver resolves the sibling linker from the same prefix before falling back to
other tools on
.Ev PATH .
.Pp
For
.Fl l
resolution,
.Nm
searches
.Fl L
paths first, then host defaults
.Pa /usr/lib
and
.Pa /usr/local/lib ,
unless
.Fl nostdlib
is given.
The
.Fl L
directories are searched in the order given, and each for
.Pa libNAME.so
and then
.Pa libNAME.a ;
a library that is for another machine than the link is passed over.
The machine is the one
.Fl m
names, or else that of the first input that is of one; looking into
libraries later does not change it.
Both regular ELF libraries and GNU ld script wrappers
.Pq Dv INPUT / Dv GROUP
are supported.
A file a wrapper names is looked for as named, then beside the wrapper,
then in the library directories;
.Fl l Ns Ar NAME
in one is searched for as on the command line, and inside
.Dv AS_NEEDED
it is used only if it defines something the link still needs, as gcc's
.Pa libgcc_s_asneeded.so
asks.
.Pp
An input is an archive if it begins as one does, whatever it is called;
ordinary and thin archives are read.
An archive that is truncated or whose member headers do not make sense is
an error that says what is wrong and at what offset, and ends the link.
.Sh OPTIONS
An option that is a word and takes a value may be written with the value
as the next argument or joined to it with
.Ql = :
.Fl -hash-style Ar gnu
and
.Fl -hash-style Ns = Ns Ar gnu
are the same.
The options that are one letter and take a value
.Pq Fl L , l , m , o , T , u , z
take it as the next argument or joined on with nothing between:
.Fl o Ar file
and
.Fl o Ns Ar file .
.Pp
An argument
.Cm @ Ns Ar file
is replaced by the arguments
.Ar file
holds, as with GNU ld: white space separates them, and within
.Ql \(aq...\(aq
or
.Ql \(dq...\(dq
does not; a backslash takes the character after it as it is.
An
.Cm @ Ns Ar file
among them is read in turn, up to 2000 files in all.
One that names no file that can be read, or a directory, stays as it is: an
input of that name.
Linux links a module of more than one object as
.Ql ld -r -o m.o @m.mod .
A word is recognised before a letter is, so
.Fl Ttext-segment
is not
.Fl T
and a script named
.Pa text-segment .
An option that needs a value and has none is an error, and exit status 2.
.Bl -tag -width "--unresolved-symbols=report-all"
.It Fl m32 , Fl m64 , Fl m Ar emulation
Select output architecture mode.
The emulations are
.Cm elf_i386
and
.Cm elf_x86_64 ,
each also under the name the Substrate GCC gives it,
.Cm elf_i386_substrate
and
.Cm elf_x86_64_substrate ;
and
.Cm elf32_x86_64 ,
x32: x86-64 code in
.Dv ELFCLASS32
objects of
.Dv EM_X86_64
with
.Dv RELA
relocations, as Linux links its x32 vDSO from objects
.Ic objcopy -O elf32-x86-64
made.
Its program is at the x86-64 base, 0x400000.
A GOT or PLT is made with x86-64's 8-byte entries, not x32's 4: a link
that imports from a shared object, which needs them, does not come out
right.
Without
.Fl m
the first input's class and machine choose.
Given more than once, the last is the one taken.
.It Fl -eh-frame-hdr
Make
.Pa .eh_frame_hdr ,
the sorted table of functions and their unwinding records that an
unwinder searches, and the
.Dv PT_GNU_EH_FRAME
header that points to it.
A C++ program that is dynamically linked cannot catch an exception
without it.
Records for functions that were left out of the output have no entry;
if the records are in a form the linker does not read, the section is
made with no table, which sends the unwinder to
.Pa .eh_frame
itself.
.It Fl -sysroot Ns = Ns Ar dir
Accepted; the libraries are found through the
.Fl L
directories the compiler driver gives.
.It Fl -copy-dt-needed-entries , Fl -no-copy-dt-needed-entries
With the first, a shared library that a named shared library needs is
looked for as
.Fl l : Ns Ar name
would be and, if it defines something the link still wants, is used and
recorded as needed too, and so on through what it needs.
This is how a C++ program linked with
.Pa libstdc++
finds the unwinder in
.Pa libgcc_s .
The default is not to.
.It Fl r
Produce relocatable
.Dv ET_REL
output.
Input sections ordered by another
.Pq Dv SHF_LINK_ORDER ,
such as gcc's
.Pa __patchable_function_entries ,
go to one output section of their name for each output section they are
ordered by, its
.Va sh_link
that section, as GNU ld makes them; so they do in a program too, unless a
script gathers them under a name of its own.
.It Fl shared
Produce a shared object: no entry is needed, what it leaves undefined is
for the program that loads it to supply, and it is not made executable.
Its global symbols of default visibility may be preempted: if the
program, or a library loaded before it, defines the same name, that
definition is used by everything, this object included.
So the object reaches its own such symbols as it would another's,
through the GOT and the PLT, by relocations that name them.
Symbols of hidden or protected visibility, and local ones, are bound when
the object is made.
.It Fl Bsymbolic , Fl Bsymbolic-functions
In a shared object, bind references to its own global symbols when it is
made; nothing can then take their place.
.It Fl pie
Produce a position-independent executable.
It is
.Dv ET_DYN
as a shared object is, and a program in every other way: it begins at
its entry, an undefined symbol is an error, it is made executable, and
.Dv DF_1_PIE
marks it.
Like any program it exports, as GNU ld's does, only what it imports, what
a shared object of the link refers to, and its copies of shared objects'
variables (with the version of the definition each copies); everything
with
.Fl -export-dynamic .
.Fl no-pie
undoes it.
.It Fl static
Use archives for the libraries that follow.
It does not change what is produced:
.Fl static Fl pie
is a PIE.
.Pp
Executables and shared objects are branded for Substrate
.Pq Dv ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE ,
whichever system the linker itself runs on; relocatable output is not.
.It Fl e Ar symbol
Where an executable begins.
A symbol named here, or by
.Ic ENTRY
in a script, that is not defined is an error.
Without either the program begins at
.Li _start ,
and if there is none at the start of
.Pa .text ,
with a warning that says so.
.It Fl -dynamic-linker Ar path
The program interpreter of the output
.Pq Dv PT_INTERP ;
on Substrate
.Pa /sbin/ld.so .
.It Fl soname Ar name , Fl h Ar name
The name a shared object gives itself
.Pq Dv DT_SONAME ,
which is the name programs linked against it will ask for.
.It Fl rpath Ar dir
A directory for the dynamic linker to search, recorded in
.Dv DT_RUNPATH ;
repeated, the directories are joined with colons.
Substrate's dynamic linker does not consult it.
.Fl rpath-link
is accepted and has no effect.
.Pp
These three may be written with one dash or two, and with their value as
the next argument or after
.Ql = .
.It Fl L Ar dir , Fl l Ar name
Add a library search directory and request a library.
.It Fl nostdlib
Search only the
.Fl L
directories for
.Fl l
and for the libraries that shared objects need, not
.Pa /usr/lib
and
.Pa /usr/local/lib .
It may be written with one dash or two.
.It Fl Bstatic , Fl Bdynamic
Toggle static/dynamic library lookup mode for subsequent
.Fl l
operands.
.It Fl -start-group ... Fl -end-group
Repeatedly scan grouped libraries until no new unresolved symbols are satisfied.
.It Fl -whole-archive , Fl -no-whole-archive
Force extraction of all archive members for subsequent
.Fl l
or archive inputs.
.It Fl -as-needed , Fl -no-as-needed
Control whether DSOs without currently unresolved symbol matches are ignored.
.It Fl T Ar script
Use a linker script; see
.Sx LINKER SCRIPTS .
.It Fl Ttext-segment Ar address
Where the image begins: the address of the first loaded segment, a
multiple of the page size.
The default is 0x08048000 for i386 and 0x400000 for x86-64, and 0 for a
shared object.
.Fl Ttext ,
.Fl Tdata
and
.Fl Tbss
are refused; a linker script says where a section goes.
.It Fl S , Fl -strip-debug , Fl s , Fl -strip-all
Leave the debugging information out of the output.
.Fl s
does no more than
.Fl S :
the symbol table is kept.
.It Fl q , Fl -emit-relocs
Keep the relocation sections in an executable or shared object.
.It Fl -build-id Ns Op = Ns Ar style
Make a
.Pa .note.gnu.build-id
note
.Pq Dv NT_GNU_BUILD_ID , owner Li GNU
in a program or shared object, placed as any note is (a script's
.Li *(.note*)
takes it), as GNU ld makes it.
The
.Ar style
is
.Cm sha1 ,
the default, a 20-byte SHA-1 of the whole output with the ID zero while
it is taken, so that the same output has the same ID;
.Cm md5 ,
16 bytes of MD5 so;
.Cm uuid ,
16 random bytes;
.Cm 0x Ns Ar hex ,
those bytes
.Pq Ql -
and
.Ql \&:
between digits are read over ;
or
.Cm none .
Without the option there is no build ID.
The digest is of this linker's output, which is not GNU ld's to the
byte, and GNU ld takes its own over what it has in memory: the two IDs
differ.
.It Fl -gc-sections
Leave out the input sections that nothing uses.
A section is used if it holds the entry or a symbol named by
.Fl -undefined ,
if it holds what the output exports
.Po
every global symbol of a shared object, or of anything linked with
.Fl -export-dynamic ;
in a program, what the shared objects of the link refer to
.Pc ,
if it runs without being called
.Po
.Pa .init ,
.Pa .fini ,
the constructor and destructor arrays
.Pc ,
if it is a note, is flagged
.Dv SHF_GNU_RETAIN ,
has a name that is a C identifier, or is named in a linker script's
.Ic KEEP ,
or if a section that is used refers to it.
.Pa .eh_frame
is kept, and a function's exception table with the function.
Sections that are not loaded are kept.
The decision is made of the inputs' sections before they are gathered,
so it is of use with objects compiled
.Fl ffunction-sections
and
.Fl fdata-sections ;
with
.Fl r
it does nothing.
.It Fl -print-gc-sections
Name each section left out by
.Fl -gc-sections ,
and the input it was in.
.It Fl -icf Ns = Ns Ar mode
Fold identical input sections into one, as gold and lld do:
two functions compiled with
.Fl ffunction-sections
to the same bytes, with relocations of the same kinds at the same
places against the same symbols, end up at one address.
A relocation against a symbol in a section that is itself identical to
another counts as one against that other, the classes being refined
until none splits, as gold does: two functions that call two identical
functions are identical too.
In a shared object a global symbol of default visibility, which may be
bound elsewhere when the object is loaded, matches only itself
(unless
.Fl Bsymbolic ) .
Template instances and inline functions, which compilers put in section
groups (COMDAT), are folded as any other code.
The inputs' sections are compared before
.Pa .text.*
is gathered into
.Pa .text .
In
.Ar safe
mode only code
.Pf ( Pa .text
and
.Pa .text.* )
is folded, and only a function whose address is not taken, as gold
decides it: a function that something does no more than call or jump
to keeps the one address its twins share, but one referred to in any
other way (a pointer to it in data, an address loaded by code, a GOT
or GOT-relative reference) keeps an address of its own, so pointers to
two functions still compare unequal; in
.Ar all
mode read-only data
.Pf ( Pa .rodata
and
.Pa .rodata.* )
is folded too.
Writable sections, mergeable, thread-local and compressed sections are
never folded, nor is code whose frame in
.Pa .eh_frame
names an LSDA (the table of what its exception handlers catch, in
.Pa .gcc_except_table ) ,
as lld leaves it: two such functions may catch different exceptions,
and the unwinder finds the table by the address it unwinds through.
.Ar none ,
the default, folds nothing.
Not with
.Fl r .
.It Fl -hash-style Ns = Ns Ar style
Select dynamic symbol hash style.
.It Fl -defsym Ar name=value
Define or override an absolute symbol.
.It Fl -undefined Ar symbol
Force a symbol into the unresolved root set.
.It Fl -allow-undefined , Fl -no-undefined
Set unresolved symbol policy.
.It Fl -unresolved-symbols Ns = Ns Ar policy
GNU-style unresolved symbol policy alias.
.It Fl -trace , Fl -trace-symbol Ns = Ns Ar symbol
Trace input loading and symbol usage.
.It Fl -z Ar option
Set text-relocation, stack-execute, and RELRO policy.
With
.Cm relro ,
the default, what only the dynamic linker writes
.Po
.Pa .data.rel.ro ,
the constructor tables,
.Pa .dynamic ,
.Pa .got
.Pc
is laid out together on pages of its own, ahead of the data the program
writes, and
.Dv PT_GNU_RELRO
covers those pages whole.
Substrate's dynamic linker makes them read-only in the libraries it
loads, not in the program the kernel loaded.
.Cm now
marks the output for the dynamic linker to bind every symbol when it is
loaded
.Pq Dv DT_BIND_NOW , DF_BIND_NOW , DF_1_NOW ;
.Cm lazy
undoes it.
.Cm max-page-size Ns = Ns Ar size ,
a power of two, is the largest page the output may be loaded in: each
.Dv PT_LOAD
is aligned to it, and its file offset falls at the same place in such a
page as its address; it is 4 KiB by default, and
.Li CONSTANT(MAXPAGESIZE)
in a script.
.Cm separate-code
puts the code on pages of its own, as GNU ld does: with no script, a
program or shared object is a read-only segment of the headers,
.Pa .interp
and the dynamic linker's tables, then one of the code, one of the
read-only data, and the writable one.
.Cm noseparate-code
makes the headers, the code and the read-only data one segment that can
be executed.
As with GNU ld, the default is
.Cm separate-code
in the linker built for a Linux host, and
.Cm noseparate-code
in Substrate's.
Any other keyword is a warning and is ignored.
.It Fl X , Fl -discard-locals , Fl x , Fl -discard-all , Fl -discard-none
Which local symbols the output leaves out, as GNU ld leaves them out.
By default these are the temporaries (names beginning
.Ql .L ,
.Ql ..
or
.Ql _.L_ ,
such as gcc's
.Li .LC0 )
of input sections that are
.Dv SHF_MERGE ,
and none under
.Fl r .
.Fl X
leaves out every temporary,
.Fl x
every local, and
.Fl -discard-none
none; under
.Fl r
a symbol a relocation names stays.
The last of them given counts.
.It Fl -warn-execstack-objects , Fl -warn-execstack , Fl -no-warn-execstack
What an executable stack is warned of, as GNU ld warns of it.
By default
.Pq Fl -warn-execstack-objects
it is the first input with an executable
.Pa .note.GNU-stack ,
named in
.Dq Ar input : requires executable stack ,
unless
.Fl z Cm execstack
or
.Fl z Cm noexecstack
decided the stack; this is said under
.Fl r
too.
.Fl -warn-execstack
also warns of the executable stack
.Fl z Cm execstack
asks for, and
.Fl -no-warn-execstack
of neither.
.It Fl -error-execstack , Fl -no-error-execstack
Whether that warning is an error instead, the link failing.
.It Fl -warn-rwx-segments , Fl -no-warn-rwx-segments
Whether a
.Dv PT_LOAD
of an executable or shared object that is readable, writable and
executable is warned of; it is, by default.
.It Fl -error-rwx-segments , Fl -no-error-rwx-segments
Whether that warning, when it is given, is an error instead.
.Pp
For each pair the last option given counts.
A link that fails takes away an output of that name from before, as GNU
ld does.
.It Fl -plugin Ar path
Set plugin executable used for LTO materialization.
A plugin that is a shared object, which is what GCC names on every link,
cannot be run and is set aside; an input that then turns out to hold LTO
bytecode is an error.
.It Fl -plugin-opt Ar option
Pass plugin-specific options.
.It Fl -Map Ar file
Write a link map including inputs, sections, program headers, and symbol provenance.
A map that cannot be written in full is an error.
.It Fl -reproduce Ar dir
Write into
.Ar dir ,
made if it is not there, what is needed to do the link again: a copy of
each object linked
.Pq Pa input_NNN.o ,
of the linker script if there was one, a
.Pa manifest.txt
of what they were, and a script
.Pa repro.sh
that links the copies into
.Pa repro.out
beside them.
The script runs the linker named by
.Ev LD_TOOL ,
or
.Nm ,
and passes on its own arguments.
Shared objects are not copied; the script names them where they were.
A bundle that cannot be written in full, or whose paths would not fit,
is an error.
.It Fl -compat Ns = Ns Ar gnu|lld
Set unsupported-option policy for compatibility behavior.
.El
.Sh SECTIONS
With no linker script an input section goes to the output section of its
own name, except that a section named
.Pa .text.NAME ,
.Pa .rodata.NAME ,
.Pa .data.rel.ro.NAME ,
.Pa .data.NAME ,
.Pa .bss.NAME ,
.Pa .tdata.NAME ,
.Pa .tbss.NAME
or
.Pa .gcc_except_table.NAME
goes to the section named without the
.Pa .NAME .
The constructor tables
.Pq Pa .init_array.N
are not gathered, and with
.Fl r
nothing is.
Where sections of strings or constants
.Pq Dv SHF_MERGE
are gathered with others the result is not marked as such.
.Sh THE GLOBAL OFFSET TABLE
.Pa .got
has a slot for each symbol that code reaches through the table: first
those imported from shared objects, which the dynamic linker fills in,
then those the output defines itself, which hold their addresses and in a
shared object or a position-independent executable have a
.Dv RELATIVE
relocation each.
A weak reference that nothing defines has a slot holding 0.
A slot is not made where the only references are loads under a
relocation that permits rewriting
.Pq Dv R_386_GOT32X , R_X86_64_GOTPCRELX , R_X86_64_REX_GOTPCRELX ;
those become the address computed in place.
A program that imports nothing still gets the table if its code is
position-independent.
.Sh INDIRECT FUNCTIONS
A symbol of type
.Dv STT_GNU_IFUNC
is a resolver: it is called once when the program starts, and what it
returns is the function.
One bound in the output is given a stub in
.Pa .iplt
that jumps through an entry in
.Pa .got ,
which the dynamic linker fills with the resolver's result
.Pq Dv R_386_IRELATIVE , R_X86_64_IRELATIVE ;
the stub is the function's address for calls, pointers and comparisons
alike, and the symbol table goes on giving the resolver's.
One that a shared object exports is reached by name, as any symbol that
may be preempted is.
A statically linked program cannot have one and is refused.
.Sh THREAD-LOCAL STORAGE
The sections flagged
.Dv SHF_TLS ,
.Pa .tdata
and then
.Pa .tbss ,
are what each thread has a copy of.
They are described by a
.Dv PT_TLS
program header, aligned as the strictest of their contents;
.Pa .tbss
takes up no room in the image, and a thread-local symbol's value is its
offset in the copy.
In a program every reference to a thread-local variable, however it was
compiled
.Pq Fl ftls-model ,
comes to an offset from the thread pointer: a constant in the
instruction for a variable the program defines, and the contents of a GOT
slot, filled in by the dynamic linker
.Pq Dv R_386_TLS_TPOFF , R_X86_64_TPOFF64 ,
for one from a shared object.
The general-dynamic and local-dynamic sequences, which call
.Fn __tls_get_addr ,
are rewritten in place into those forms and the call removed, so a
program does not need that function.
On x86-64 the constant is a sign-extended 32-bit field
.Pq Dv R_X86_64_TPOFF32 , No and Dv R_X86_64_GOTTPOFF No rewritten to it ,
so a variable more than 2 GiB from the thread pointer is an error
.Pq Dq relocation truncated to fit ,
not a distance cut short.
.Pp
A shared object cannot know which module it will be or how far its
storage will be from the thread pointer, so its references to its own
thread-local variables go through entries in
.Pa .got ,
after the ordinary slots, for the dynamic linker to fill in: a pair,
module and offset, for a general-dynamic reference
.Pq Dv R_386_TLS_DTPMOD32 , R_X86_64_DTPMOD64 ;
one such pair for all local-dynamic references; and one entry holding the
distance from the thread pointer for an initial-exec reference
.Pq Dv R_386_TLS_TPOFF , R_X86_64_TPOFF64 ,
which also marks the object
.Dv DF_STATIC_TLS .
Local-exec code, which has the distance built in, cannot go into a
shared object and is reported as an error.
.Sh SYMBOL VERSIONS
A symbol an input object names
.Li sym@VER
or
.Li sym@@VER
.Pq the assembler's Li .symver
is exported from a shared object as
.Li sym
of version
.Li VER ,
the second form making it the default version.
The versions are listed in
.Pa .gnu.version_d
after a base version named for the object, and each dynamic symbol's
version is in
.Pa .gnu.version .
A reference to plain
.Li sym
binds to the first shared object that defines it, to its default version
if it has versions, and records that version as needed
.Pq Pa .gnu.version_r ;
a version that is not the default satisfies only a reference that names
it.
.Pp
A version script, read from
.Fl -version-script Ar file
or a linker script's
.Ic VERSION { } ,
is a list of version nodes,
.Ql NAME { global: patterns; local: patterns; } PARENT; ,
an anonymous one being
.Ql { ... }; .
A defined global or weak symbol that a node's global patterns match is
given that node's version, the default one
.Pq Li name@@NAME ;
one that a local pattern matches is made local and not exported.
An exact name is taken over a pattern
.Pq Li * , ? , [...] ,
and a pattern over
.Li * ;
names under
.Li extern \(dqC\(dq
are matched as any others, and names under
.Li extern \(dqC++\(dq
(or any other language) are read over.
Every named node is a version defined in
.Pa .gnu.version_d ,
in the script's order, with the one it depends on, weak if it names
nothing, and an absolute symbol of its name, as GNU ld defines them.
.Sh LINKER SCRIPTS
The script named by
.Fl T
is read once, before anything is linked, and a script that cannot be read
is reported with its file, line and column and ends the link.
The commands are those of GNU ld.
Understood and acted on:
.Bl -tag -width "INCLUDE file"
.It Ic SECTIONS
Output section descriptions, each
.Dq name [address] : [ALIGN(n)] { ... } [>region] [:phdr ...] ,
and between them assignments,
.Ic PROVIDE ,
.Ic ASSERT
and
.Ic INCLUDE .
An input section goes to the first output section whose body asks for it
by file and section pattern, with the wildcards
.Ql * ,
.Ql \&?
and
.Ql [...] ;
.Ic EXCLUDE_FILE
is honoured,
.Ic KEEP
protects the section from
.Fl -gc-sections ,
and
.Ic /DISCARD/
leaves what it names out.
A
.Ic /DISCARD/
of
.Li .dynamic
leaves out all the dynamic tables the linker would make,
.Li .dynamic , .dynsym , .dynstr , .hash
and
.Li .gnu.hash ,
as GNU ld does: Linux's x86-64 decompressor is linked
.Fl pie
by a script that discards them.
The sections a description takes go in in the order of the inputs,
except those a pattern inside
.Ic SORT
or
.Ic SORT_BY_NAME
takes, which go in by name,
.Ic SORT_BY_ALIGNMENT ,
the most aligned first, and
.Ic SORT_BY_INIT_PRIORITY ,
by the number their name ends in
.Pq of Li .ctors No and Li .dtors , No the other way ;
of two nested, the outer sorts and the inner breaks its ties, and
otherwise ties keep the order of the inputs, as in GNU ld.
.Ic SORT_NONE
sorts nothing.
An output section the script names is placed at the location counter,
however it is protected: no page is skipped between read-only, code and
data, as GNU ld does not skip one; a script that wants them apart says so
with
.Ic ALIGN .
An input section nothing asks for keeps its own name, and is placed where
the default layout would have put it among the sections the script names,
on a page of its own when it is protected unlike the section before it.
.It Ic PHDRS
The program headers of the output.
A section goes in the headers named after its output section, and one
that names none goes where the one before it went, counting an output
section the script names that nothing went into, as GNU ld does.
A
.Dv PT_LOAD
without
.Ic FLAGS
has the permissions of the sections in it.
Without
.Ic PHDRS ,
a
.Dv PT_LOAD
holds a run of sections of one kind (code, read-only data, writable
data) that are one run of memory, as GNU ld makes them: a section begins
another where it is loaded at another distance from its address than
the one before it
.Pq Ic AT ,
or where it is more than a page past where that one ends.
.It Ic MEMORY
Regions that
.Dq >region
places sections in; a section that does not fit is an error.
.It Ic ENTRY
The entry symbol, unless
.Fl e
names one.
.It Ic INCLUDE
Another script, read where the command stands as if its text stood there.
The file is looked for beside the including script, then as named, then
in the
.Fl L
directories.
.It Ic ASSERT
An error with the given message when its expression is zero.
The semicolon after it is optional.
.El
.Pp
Inside
.Ic SECTIONS
the location counter
.Ql \&.
is the address the next thing is placed at.
It begins at 0, as in GNU ld, and may only move forwards.
Assigned between output sections it places what follows; in a body it
places what follows it in the section, and moved past the last input
section description it makes the section longer.
In a body, as in GNU ld,
.Ql \&.
and a symbol in the section are addresses in it, and an operator between
one of them and a plain number, or between two of them, works on their
offsets in the section:
.Dq ". = sym | (1 << 2);"
sets a bit of
.Ar sym Ns 's
offset.
A number, and a symbol that is absolute (one the body set with
.Ic ABSOLUTE
too), are plain numbers there, and a
plain number assigned to
.Ql \&.
or a symbol is an offset in the section:
.Dq "x = ABSOLUTE(.); y = x;"
makes
.Ar y
the section's address plus
.Ar x .
.Dq ALIGN(n)
in a body aligns the address, and
.Dq ALIGN(exp, n)
of an address in the section aligns its offset there, as GNU ld does;
neither makes the section more aligned than its input sections make it.
.Ic ASSERT
tests an address in a section as its offset in it.
Where the location counter is moved to what the linker cannot know while
it puts the input sections together, such as a symbol's address, the
link is done over with the addresses the pass before came to, until they
settle, as GNU ld sizes its sections over.
.Pp
The data statements
.Ic BYTE ,
.Ic SHORT ,
.Ic LONG ,
.Ic QUAD
and
.Ic SQUAD
put the value of their expression, of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes in the output's
byte order, at the location counter and move it past them.
They may stand after an output section's last input section description,
or be all a section has, which is then made for them; before or between
input section descriptions they are refused.
.Ic SIZEOF
counts them.
.Pp
A symbol is defined by
.Dq name = expression ;
or changed by
.Dq name += expression ;
and the other compound operators.
.Ic PROVIDE
defines its symbol only if it is referred to and nothing defines it, as
GNU ld has it: by an input, or by an expression of the script, as in
.Dq "PROVIDE(q = 7); x = q;" ,
an
.Ic ASSERT ,
a data statement or another
.Ic PROVIDE
that is itself referred to, though not by
.Ic DEFINED ;
.Ic HIDDEN
and
.Ic PROVIDE_HIDDEN
make the symbol hidden.
A symbol set with
.Ql =
in an output section's body is where GNU ld puts it: in that section for
.Ql \&.
or a plain number, in the other section for a symbol or
.Ic ADDR
of one, and absolute for an absolute address, so
.Dq "setup_size = ALIGN(ABSOLUTE(.), 4096);"
is absolute; in an output section nothing went into, which is not made,
what would be in it goes in the section before it or the one after,
whichever is more like it, of two alike the one after if the symbol is
not before it, as GNU ld chooses.
Any other symbol defined inside
.Ic SECTIONS
belongs to the section its address falls in (in an output section's body,
that section, counting what its body adds to its end), except one set with
.Ql =
between output sections or outside
.Ic SECTIONS ,
which is where GNU ld puts it: a symbol or
.Ic ADDR
in the expression is an address in its section, a number or
.Ql \&.
an absolute address, and
.Ic SIZEOF ,
.Ic ALIGNOF
or a comparison a plain number; of two operands that differ a plain
number takes the other's section and otherwise the result is absolute, as
arithmetic on two of one kind is, so
.Dq "sym = other + 1;"
is absolute and
.Dq "sym = other + SIZEOF(.text);"
is in other's section; and what came from
.Ql \&.
alone, unless
.Ic ABSOLUTE
took it out, is in the section before it, or the next one if
.Ql \&.
was set since.
One changed with an operator outside
.Ic SECTIONS
is absolute.
A symbol set with
.Ql =
to an expression that names one symbol of the program, and no other,
takes that symbol's type, as in GNU ld:
.Dq "PROVIDE(alias = func);"
is a function.
Expressions have the operators of C, parentheses nested no more than 256
deep, and the functions
.Ic ALIGN
.Po
with one argument the location counter rounded up to a multiple of it,
with two the first rounded up to a multiple of the second
.Pc ,
.Ic ADDR ,
.Ic LOADADDR ,
.Ic SIZEOF ,
.Ic ALIGNOF ,
.Ic DEFINED ,
.Ic MAX ,
.Ic MIN ,
.Ic ABSOLUTE ,
.Ic ORIGIN
and
.Ic LENGTH
of a memory region,
.Ic CONSTANT
of
.Dv MAXPAGESIZE
or
.Dv COMMONPAGESIZE
.Pq both 4096 ,
.Ic SIZEOF_HEADERS ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_ALIGN ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_END ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_RELRO_END
and
.Ic SEGMENT_START
.Pq always its default .
Any other function is an error that names it.
A number may end in
.Ql K
or
.Ql M .
Of
.Dq c \&? a : b
only the arm that is taken is evaluated, so that
.Dq DEFINED(x) \&? x : 0
is not an error when
.Ar x
is undefined.
A name in an expression is a symbol the script defines or one of the
program's, whose value is its address; a name that is neither is an
error.
.Ic DEFINED
is true of both kinds.
A statement may use a symbol that a later statement of the script
defines.
.Pp
Every gap in an output section, the padding an input section's
alignment leaves and the room an assignment to
.Ql \&.
makes, is filled with the fill in effect where it is: the last
.Ic FILL Ns Pq Ar expr
before it in the section's body, else the section's
.Ql = Ns Ar fill ,
else no-ops in code
.Pq what GNU ld's x86 back end writes
and zeros elsewhere, by what the output section is once all its input
sections are in it.
A fill written as a hex number is that string of bytes, as long as it
is written
.Po
.Ql 0x123
is the bytes 01 23
.Pc ;
any other expression is worked out as the script is read, a
nonconstant one being an error, and is 4 bytes, most significant first.
Each gap starts the pattern over.
.Pp
Read and not acted on:
.Ic OUTPUT_FORMAT ,
.Ic OUTPUT_ARCH ,
.Ic OUTPUT ,
.Ic TARGET ,
.Ic SEARCH_DIR ,
.Ic INPUT ,
.Ic GROUP ,
.Ic STARTUP ,
.Ic EXTERN ,
.Ic INSERT ,
.Ic NOCROSSREFS ,
.Ic REGION_ALIAS ,
the load address of a
.Ic PHDRS
entry
.Pq Ic AT No in Ic PHDRS ,
an output section's load region
.Pq Ic AT> Ns Ar region ,
.Ic BLOCK
and
.Ic SUBALIGN .
An output section's
.Ic AT Ns Pq Ar lma ,
a script's
.Ic VERSION
and the data statements are acted on, as told above.
.Sh OUTPUT
The output is written beside its name and moved into place when it is
whole, so that an existing file of that name is replaced at once or not
at all.
A name that is not a plain file is written into instead: a link is
followed, and a device or a pipe receives the output and stays what it
was.
A file the linker makes has the mode a new file has, less the umask, and
is executable if it is a program.
.Pp
In a program or shared object the sections that are
.Dv SHF_MERGE
.Po
.Pa .rodata.str1.* ,
.Pa .rodata.cst* ,
.Pa .comment ,
.Pa .debug_str
.Pc
are merged as GNU ld merges them: those of one output section alike in
entry size, strings or not, and alignment are laid out as one block that
holds each string or constant once, and a string that ends another is
found at that one's end.
A section with relocations of its own is not merged.
A block that would not fit in the linker's address space, as on a 32-bit
host the strings of many sections each aligned to a large boundary may
not, fails the link.
Under
.Fl r
nothing is merged.
.Pp
On x86 the inputs'
.Pa .note.gnu.property
notes are merged into one, as GNU ld merges them: a property of the AND
kind (the IBT and SHSTK bits of
.Dv GNU_PROPERTY_X86_FEATURE_1_AND )
is ANDed and gone if any input lacks it, one of the OR kind (the ISA
needed, the ISA and features used) is ORed, and one that comes out 0 is
dropped; with none left there is no note.
This is done under
.Fl r
as well.
An input's notes are read up to the first note or property whose size
runs past the end of its note or section; what follows it is ignored.
.Pp
With no script's
.Ic SECTIONS ,
the link defines the symbols GNU ld's default script does:
.Va __bss_start
(where the zeros begin),
.Va _edata
(the end of what has contents) and
.Va _end
(the end of the image, aligned to a word) in every program, and in a
shared object where something refers to them; and
.Va edata ,
.Va end ,
.Va etext ,
.Va _etext ,
.Va __etext
(the end of the code) and
.Va __executable_start
(the image's first address) where something refers to them and no input
defines them.
.Pp
The symbol table of a program or shared object has no section symbols,
as GNU ld's has none, unless
.Fl -emit-relocs
keeps relocations that name them; under
.Fl r
they stay.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics are emitted in parser-friendly one-line forms such as
.Dq ld: error: ...
and
.Dq ld: warning: ... .
For select classes,
.Nm
adds structured notes with category/source/hint metadata.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY NOTES
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm
implements GNU-compatible options for the supported feature set but is not a full GNU ld script superset;
.Sx LINKER SCRIPTS
says what of a script is acted on.
.It
Input sections stored compressed
.Pq Dv SHF_COMPRESSED , zlib
are linked as what they hold and written uncompressed.
Sections that are not loaded, the debugging information among them, are
relocated like the rest, and an executable or shared object carries no
relocation sections of the linker's own.
.It
A shared library is recorded as needed under its own
.Dv DT_SONAME ,
not under the name it was found by.
.It
In an executable, a weak reference that neither an input nor a shared
object of the link defines is zero at link time.
.It
An executable's dynamic symbol table has what it imports, what the
shared objects of the link refer to or define and it defines too, its
copies of their variables, and with
.Fl -export-dynamic
everything global.
.It
A shared object's references to its own thread-local variables are bound
when it is linked; a definition of the same name elsewhere does not take
their place.
.It
In an executable made of code that is not position-independent, a call
to a function in a shared object goes through the PLT; a function whose
address is taken has its PLT entry for an address, the same in every
module; and a variable of a shared object gets a copy in the executable
.Pq Pa .dynbss , Dv R_*_COPY ,
under its name and, as GNU ld pairs them, a weak name and a strong one
the shared object has for the same place; two strong names there are two
variables, each copied by itself.
A PIE gets such a copy of a variable it refers to pc-relatively, as gcc
compiles a PIE for x86-64 to do; one it refers to only by absolute
address is relocated where it is referred to instead.
A thread-local variable referred to that way is an error.
.It
.Fl z Cm text
refuses an output in which the dynamic linker would have to apply a
relocation to read-only memory, which is what a shared object made of
code that is not position-independent comes to.
Without it such an output is marked
.Dv DT_TEXTREL
and a warning says so;
.Fl z Cm notext
silences the warning.
Substrate's dynamic linker does not apply relocations to read-only
memory.
.It
GNU plugin shared-object API is not loaded directly;
.Fl -plugin
expects an executable materializer interface.
.It
Behavior for unsupported options differs by
.Fl -compat
mode.
.El
